  "query_preview": "..The atmospheric river (AR) event continues this \nmorning across southeast Arizona, aided by a strong subtropical jet \njust ahead of a strong upper level trough. Rainfall amounts so far \nhave overperformed a bit for much of the region with most locales \nseeing 0.25\" to 1 inch of rain in the last 24 hours. Lesser amounts \nhave occurred in Cochise County and higher amounts of 2 to 3 inches \nhave been observed on Mt. Lemmon and Mt. Graham. Latest KEMX radar \nimagery shows most locales remaining dry southeast of Tucson with a \nsolid area of light to moderate rain with localized pockets of heavy \nrain from approximately the Tucson area and points north/west. This \nprecipitation orientation will continue through about mid morning \nwith the CAMs in good agreement on the precipitation shield mainly \nstaying from the Tucson area north and westward through about 9 AM. \nAs the day progresses, a band of moderate rain with pockets of heavy \nrain with embedded thunderstorms will shift from west to east across \nthe region, exiting eastern areas this evening. This band will \nproduce rainfall rates around 0.25\" to 0.50\" per hour (locally \nhigher in any thunderstorms) as it moves through in a 4 to 6 hour \nperiod. For some context, the atmosphere is at record levels of \nmoisture (1.2\" to 1.6\" PWAT or 5 to 6 standard deviations above \nnormal) for this time of year. Thus, the combination of the dynamics \nwith the subtropical jet max along with moisture levels more \ntypically seen in summer will continue to result in efficient \nrainfall. While the bulk of the precipitation will occur through the \nday and into the evening, some localized pockets of moderate rain may \nstill occur tonight into Monday morning. This will be along a \ntrailing secondary band of precipitation aided by the upper level \ntrough across the northern half of Arizona with this band slowly \npush south and east, focused on areas north and west of Tucson \ntonight.\n\nWhile there will be potential for some embedded thunderstorms today, \nlimited instability will be a significant mitigating factor. SPC has \noutlined much of our forecast area in a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) \nfor severe thunderstorms with wind being the main threat as stronger \nwinds aloft can translate down to the surface in any stronger \nthunderstorms. Even outside of thunderstorm activity, breezy \nsouthwest winds are expected today. \n\nAdditional most likely rain amounts from 5 AM this morning to 5 AM \nMonday continue to range from about 0.3\" to 1.2\" in valleys with \nlocalized amounts of 2 to 3 inches mainly in valleys north of Tucson \nand in the mountains from Tucson northeastward. This additional \nprecipitation will continue to result in responses in area rivers, \ncreeks, and washes across SE AZ. See hydrology section below for \nfurther details. \n\nHere is a look at updated additional rainfall ranges from 5 AM \nSunday to 5 AM Monday. \n\n-Expected: Valleys 0.30\"-1.2\", Mountains 1\"-3\"   \n\n-Low end (9 in 10 chance):\n Valleys 0.10\"-1.0\", Mountains 0.75\"-2\" \n  \n-High end (1 in 10 chance): \n Valleys 1.2\"-2.5\", Mountains 3\"-4\" locally higher\n\nLingering showers will diminish Monday morning with no significant \nrainfall expected. Thereafter, dry conditions as shortwave ridging \nbuilds in. This shortwave ridging will traverse across the regions \nTuesday with dry conditions still continuing beyond Wednesday and \nThursday as westerly flow prevails. Some semblance of troughing \naround Friday but still some differences in the ensembles and \nmoisture is lacking so will keep PoPs below mentionable levels into \nnext weekend. Temperatures will be 4 to 7 degrees below normal for \nmost of this week.",
